  "query_preview": "..\n(This afternoon through Monday)\nIssued at 135 PM CDT TUE AUG 25 2026\n\nA weak front has pushed southward and brought drier air to the\nregion today. Aside from our far southeastern counties, where a\nfew showers and storms may develop this afternoon, most of the\nforecast area will remain dry through tonight, with PWAT values\ninsufficient for diurnal convection. \n\nMoisture will gradually increase Wednesday into Thursday as a \nvery broad upper-level trough, enhanced by MCS activity, \napproaches from the west. The combination of increasing lift and \nmoisture should lead to increasingly widespread showers and storms \nWednesday night through Friday, with somewhat limited \npredictability regarding the timing and placement of multiple \nrounds of convection. However, there is a general model consensus \nfor the upper-level trough and associated weak surface front to \nmove slowly eastward across Alabama. This should place the highest\nrain chances along and east of Interstate 59 on Friday. \n\nRain chances should trend lower for the weekend as the trough \nshears out and the focus for convection weakens. This may lead to \na warming trend if the 500 mb ridge builds across the region as \nindicated by some model guidance.\n\n87/Grantham",
